{
  "dom_id": "step-q182666",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Prove that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{n}})$ using mathematical induction.\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich equals the right-hand side. Thus, our statement is {{field-3}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-4}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\,{{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}})+{{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}})+\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}} & ={{field-7}} \\\\  & ={{field-8}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{n}})$ is true for all {{field-9}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{n}})\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{1}})=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{3})=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ \n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{k}}).\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**, so let's write that, too.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}}).\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving sums of finite series using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to write the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}{a}_{i}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{a}_{i})+{a}_{k+1}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}})+\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}}.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}})+\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{k}})+\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{3}^{k}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{{3}^{k}}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{{3}^{k}}(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{{3}^{k}}(\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{n}})$ is true for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nPROOF\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,$$1$.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich equals the right-hand side. Thus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ $k\\ge 1$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\,(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{k}}).\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}})+\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}}.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}})+\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{k}})+\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{k+1}}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{3}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{n}})$ is true for all $n\\ge 1$.",
